  "live_records": {
    "q-344108": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344108",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205687/assets/84deebb7aa20aee2bb0b9e57db825fbdfac1482ca4aec7202ba5458c5603492f.png)\n\nThe function $t(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${t}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $t(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$\n\nGiven that $t(2)=3,$ what is the maximum value ${t}_{\\text{max}}$ of $t(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${t}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $t(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${t}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${t}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $t$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(2) & =3 \\\\ t(-4) & =t(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =t(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3-(-8) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $t$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $t$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{t}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $t$ is ${t}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $t(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
