    "q-344100": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344100",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205687/assets/955cda48e8e98f46903a471ceefa657a6032cb2e6feab304569a62750eba9825.png)\n\nThe function $r(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,7].$ The graph of ${r}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $r(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$\n\nGiven that $r(7)=6,$ what is the maximum value ${r}_{\\text{max}}$ of $r(x)$ on the interval $[-1,7]?$\n\n${r}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $r(x)$ on $[-1,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${r}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,7),$ and ${r}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $r$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(7) & =6 \\\\ r(-1) & =r(7)+{\\int }_{7}^{-1}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =r(7)-{\\int }_{-1}^{7}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6-(-10) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $r$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $r$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{6,16\\}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $r$ is ${r}_{\\text{max}}=16,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $r(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
