    "q-316301": {
      "dom_id": "step-q316301",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205687/assets/bcffe721c35a3c729644fb36ee9008095c46298a56109f0dfd75a3c06f3f7cae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right).\n\nGiven that $f(2)=-3,$ what is the maximum value ${f}_{\\text{max}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(4+(-2)) \\\\  & =-3-2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,-1,-5\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
