    "q-104045": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104045",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205687/assets/f757ffa45b20e043ad461cb0dfa9c486cf73466b1d8d87fa0ffad00bc01af960.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $1,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5]$ given that $f(5)=-8.$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =-8 \\\\ f(3) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{3}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8-1 \\\\  & =-9 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8-(-4+1) \\\\  & =-8-(-3) \\\\  & =-8+3 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-8,-9,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
