    "q-344167": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344167",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205687/assets/6dd61819859d65af85ef462c7fb694ecbfd2640aa62f41ff0e27e5bb8073f48c.png)\n\nThe function $a(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${a}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $a(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $11.$\n\nGiven that $a(5)=-4,$ what is the minimum value ${a}_{\\text{min}}$ of $a(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]?$\n\n${a}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $a(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${a}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${a}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $a$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(5) & =-4 \\\\ a(-2) & =a(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =a(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4-(-11) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $a$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $a$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{7,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $a$ is ${a}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $a(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
