    "q-344182": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344182",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205687/assets/04e4299da4d4c29cce751d04ee6ee29380a3dead3ab3456f6645858b5d9d106b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(3)=2,$ what is the minimum value ${f}_{\\text{min}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205687/assets/c30d7e1d2c9eb939726d85b0ee5d51a2bdc0cfe73fab92f0ba29839a9f18be4e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-7.5 \\\\  & =-5.5 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-3+7.5) \\\\  & =2-4.5 \\\\  & =-2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,-5.5,-2.5\\}=-5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$",
